      "document_hash": "342187631083a1b5b4d1afbb03b84830fefcfb944b33539ae80731feebce5105",
      "evidence_id": "ev-342187631083-0000-0004",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:59 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Brunn W. Roysdon, Solicitor General)",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1009",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "342187631083a1b5b4d1afbb03b84830fefcfb944b33539ae80731feebce5105",
      "evidence_id": "ev-342187631083-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The issue presented in this case is fundamentally a question of statutory interpretation. When Congress enacted 2254(e)(2) as part of AEDPA, it created a high bar for federal evidentiary hearings on habeas claims involving state convictions."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1009",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "342187631083a1b5b4d1afbb03b84830fefcfb944b33539ae80731feebce5105",
      "evidence_id": "ev-342187631083-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: Counsel, the -- it seems rather odd that you would -- we would allow a -- we will excuse a default under Martinez but not allow the prisoner to make his underlying claim or develop his evidence -- evidentiary basis for his underlying claim."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1009",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "342187631083a1b5b4d1afbb03b84830fefcfb944b33539ae80731feebce5105",
      "evidence_id": "ev-342187631083-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 15,
        "start": 15
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: Counsel, the problem is that the statute doesn't define what \"at fault\" means. It just says so long as you fail to develop. So, by definition, what constitutes fault is defined by us, correct?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1009",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "342187631083a1b5b4d1afbb03b84830fefcfb944b33539ae80731feebce5105",
      "evidence_id": "ev-342187631083-0000-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: If the court in Martinez had accepted the prisoner's argument that there is a constitutional right, a Sixth Amendment right to the effective assistance of counsel in the first post-conviction proceeding when the state says you can't raise ineffective assistance of counsel until the first post-conviction proceeding, then it would follow, would it not, that the -- the fault of the ineffective attorney would not be attributed to the prisoner?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1009",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "342187631083a1b5b4d1afbb03b84830fefcfb944b33539ae80731feebce5105",
      "evidence_id": "ev-342187631083-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 28,
        "start": 28
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: I have one question, counsel."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1009",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "342187631083a1b5b4d1afbb03b84830fefcfb944b33539ae80731feebce5105",
      "evidence_id": "ev-342187631083-0001-0004",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 31,
        "start": 31
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ORAL ARGUMENT OF ROBERT M. LOEB ON BEHALF OF THE RESPONDENT"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1009",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "342187631083a1b5b4d1afbb03b84830fefcfb944b33539ae80731feebce5105",
      "evidence_id": "ev-342187631083-0001-0003",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And that's why, in Martinez and Trevino, this Court clearly anticipated that those -- these important substantial ineffective trial counsel claims would be developed once cause was -- was found and that a person was found not to be at fault for failing to raise it."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1009",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "342187631083a1b5b4d1afbb03b84830fefcfb944b33539ae80731feebce5105",
      "evidence_id": "ev-342187631083-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: But what does -- what issue specifically do you think the Court left open in Coleman? Was it the question whether the Sixth Amendment would apply in the first post-conviction proceeding, or was it the question whether there could be a non-constitutional basis for finding that the fault of the attorney is not attributable to the client?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "former DOJ and bipartisan prosecutors",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1009",
      "confidence": 0.85,
      "document_hash": "342187631083a1b5b4d1afbb03b84830fefcfb944b33539ae80731feebce5105",
      "evidence_id": "ev-342187631083-0002-0004",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 52,
        "start": 52
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "A construction of the statute that would require that, as the amicus brief from the former DOJ and bipartisan prosecutors says, that would really taint the federal judicial system."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1009",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "342187631083a1b5b4d1afbb03b84830fefcfb944b33539ae80731feebce5105",
      "evidence_id": "ev-342187631083-0002-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 53
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: Counsel, I guess, given the predictions of the dissent in Martinez, I was surprised that one of the statistics I read is that there's only two cases a year that present a Martinez hearing, where a has court found that a prisoner's eligible for a Martinez hearing."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1009",
      "confidence": 0.0,
